Certainly! Here is a JavaScript program that utilizes advanced features such as Promises, async/await, and the spread operator:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
  constructor(urls) {
    this.urls = urls;
  }

  async fetchData(url) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to fetch ${url}`);
      return await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return null;
    }
  }

  async getAllData() {
    const promises = this.urls.map(url => this.fetchData(url));
    return await Promise.all(promises);
  }
}

(async () => {
  const urls = [
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1',
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/2',
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/3'
  ];

  const fetcher = new DataFetcher(urls);
  const data = await fetcher.getAllData();

  const allTitles = data
    .filter(item => item !== null)
    .map(item => item.title);

  console.log(...allTitles);
})();
```

This program defines a `DataFetcher` class that fetches JSON data from a list of URLs. It uses `async/await` for asynchronous operations, handles errors gracefully, and logs the titles of the fetched posts using the spread operator.